From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 06:13:33 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] gdi32: a metafile measures glyph-index text as glyphs (9760)

An enhanced metafile -- what every print job is spooled as -- recorded
ETO_GLYPH_INDEX text given without widths with the advances of the
characters whose codes equal the glyph indices (glyph 0x24 measured as
'$'), so text a program shapes itself (Uniscribe, DirectWrite-to-GDI) and
prints without a width array came out spread apart and overlapping. The
glyphs are now measured as glyphs (GetTextExtentPointI), as Windows does.
Gate test/pstext-gate.sh (metafile and glyph-index parts); mutant
SG_MUTANT_EMF_GLYPH_AS_CHAR.
---
--- a/dlls/gdi32/emfdc.c
+++ b/dlls/gdi32/emfdc.c
@@ -1404,7 +1404,8 @@
         UINT i;
         SIZE str_size;
         memcpy( (char*)emr + emr->emrtext.offDx, dx, dx_len * sizeof(INT) );
-        if (GetTextExtentPoint32W( hdc, str, count, &str_size ))
+        if (flags & ETO_GLYPH_INDEX ? GetTextExtentPointI( hdc, (const WORD *)str, count, &str_size )
+                                    : GetTextExtentPoint32W( hdc, str, count, &str_size ))
             text_height = str_size.cy;
         if (flags & ETO_PDY)
         {
@@ -1427,9 +1428,16 @@
         UINT i;
         INT *emf_dx = (INT *)((char*)emr + emr->emrtext.offDx);
         SIZE charSize;
+        /* the advance of each glyph: with ETO_GLYPH_INDEX, str holds glyph indices, not
+           characters (measuring them as characters spread Uniscribe-shaped text in print) */
         for (i = 0; i < count; i++)
         {
+#ifdef SG_MUTANT_EMF_GLYPH_AS_CHAR
             if (GetTextExtentPoint32W( hdc, str + i, 1, &charSize ))
+#else
+            if (flags & ETO_GLYPH_INDEX ? GetTextExtentPointI( hdc, (const WORD *)str + i, 1, &charSize )
+                                        : GetTextExtentPoint32W( hdc, str + i, 1, &charSize ))
+#endif
             {
                 emf_dx[i] = charSize.cx;
                 text_width += charSize.cx;
